{"surface": "inspector", "title": "Passage", "html": "<header class=\"hero\"><p class=\"kicker\">Passage</p><h2 class=\"title\">benzinga.com</h2><p class=\"ctx\"><a href=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/tech/26/11/62868935/what-is-going-on-with-apple-stock-on-wednesday-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">benzinga.com/markets/tech/26/11/62868935/what-is-going-on-with-apple-stock-on-wednesday-2</a> · read Nov 19, 2026</p></header><div class=\"page md\" data-md=\"# What Is Going On With Apple Stock on Wednesday?\n\n**Apple Inc.** [(NASDAQ:](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AAPL)[AAPL](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AAPL)) shares jumped about 5% in early trading Wednesday as investors returned to mega-cap technology stocks and focused on the company’s long-term AI, Services and ecosystem opportunities despite near-term pressure from rising memory costs.\n\nThe S&amp;P 500 rose about 1.4%.\n\n## Investors Revisit the Long-Term Apple Thesis\n\n[Recent bullish commentary](https://www.benzinga.com/markets/tech/26/11/62809108/apple-is-the-mack-daddy-of-tech-says-longtime-bull-who-defends-its-steep-valuation) has kept attention on Apple’s cash-flow generation, installed base and potential AI monetization.\n\nSome longtime bulls have argued that Apple’s Services and AI opportunities could support a sum-of-the-parts valuation of roughly \\$400 to \\$450 per share over time.\n\nAt the same time, investors continue to weigh management’s warning that memory costs could rise beyond the December quarter, while supply constraints may affect Mac and iPad into next year.\n\n## BofA Sees Short-Term Pressure, Longer-Term Opportunity\n\nAfter Apple reported earnings, **BofA Securities** senior IT hardware analyst Wamsi Mohan said rising memory prices are pressuring gross margins, but he viewed Apple’s performance as relatively solid given the cost environment.\n\nMohan [told](https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/11/03/theres-a-short-term-headwind-longer-term-opportunity-for-apple-bofas-mohan.html) CNBC that Apple continues to benefit from tariff savings, lower-cost inventory and reductions in non-memory component costs.\n\nHe noted that Apple raised iPhone prices by about \\$100, less than the roughly \\$200 increase some had expected.\n\nMohan sees the margin pressure as a near-term challenge but believes higher pricing could eventually support profitability once memory costs normalize, particularly across future iPhone replacement cycles.\n\nHe also supported Apple’s decision to build inventory and secure longer-term memory contracts as hyperscalers increasingly compete for the same components.\n\nThe key uncertainty, he said, is whether higher prices eventually weaken consumer demand.\n\n## T. Rowe Price Sees Market-Share Gains\n\nAfter the results, **T. Rowe Price** portfolio manager Tony Wang said Apple is maintaining consumer demand more effectively than competitors despite higher memory costs.\n\nWang [told](https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/11/03/apple-is-proving-consumer-demand-remains-resilient-says-t-rowe-priceas-tony-wang.html) CNBC that weaker shipments across the broader industry could allow Apple to gain market share, particularly because some rivals have less ability to absorb higher component costs.\n\nHe believes lower memory prices could eventually provide an additional margin tailwind while Apple continues expanding its installed base and ecosystem.\n\nWang also cautioned that memory remains a cyclical industry and said sufficiently high prices could eventually hurt demand or push customers to redesign products around expensive components.\n\nStill, he said Apple’s ability to preserve shipments and navigate higher input costs supports its longer-term growth prospects.\n\n## ETF Exposure\n\nApple carries significant weights in the **Invesco S&amp;P 500 Momentum ETF** [(NYSE:](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/SPMO)[SPMO](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/SPMO)) at 9.70%, **Principal U.S. Mega-Cap ETF** [(NASDAQ:](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/USMC)[USMC](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/USMC)) at 9.89% and **MicroSectors FANG+ 3x Leveraged ETN** [(NYSE:](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/FNGU)[FNGU](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/FNGU)) at 9.65%.\n\nLarge inflows or outflows in those products could translate into corresponding buying or selling of Apple shares.\n\n## Price Action\n\n**AAPL Stock Price Activity:** Apple shares are trading higher by 4.94% at \\$348.30 at the time of publication on Wednesday, according to [Benzinga Pro data](https://www.benzinga.com/pro/register-3?utm_content=editorial&amp;utm_ad_type=content-text).\n\n*Image via Shutterstock*\n\n**Read Also: [What&#x27;s Going On With Alibaba Stock Wednesday?](https://www.benzinga.com/markets/tech/26/11/62861180/whats-going-on-with-alibaba-stock-wednesday-5)**\" data-base=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/tech/26/11/62868935/what-is-going-on-with-apple-stock-on-wednesday-2\"></div><p class=\"raw\"><a class=\"rawlink\" data-go=\"raw:src:185\">Full details</a></p>", "wide": true}